-design must include a mechanism for USING periodicity knowledge, not just learning it. Candidate mechanisms: (a) PGFT prompt-guided fine-tuning, (b) path/distance encoding as explicit features, (c) architecture that processes periodic topology (Chemprop+periodic edges).", "H3 (Periodicity-Aware Pre-training): REVISED — PA contrastive pre-training provides variance reduction but NOT mean improvement on simple GIN. Monomer-trimer pairs are easy positives (cos_sim 0.94-0.97) providing redundant contrastive signal. The periodicity prior must be encoded differently: through representation (periodic graph with appropriate architecture), through fine-tuning strategy (PGFT), or through explicit features (path/distance/Bloch spectrum).", "H4 (Representation-Usage Gap): NEW — The 50→30 gap is fundamentally a representation-USAGE gap, not a representation-LEARNING gap. Simple GIN can learn periodicity-aware representations (PerioGT-Lite variance reduction confirms) but cannot use them for Tg prediction (mean RMSE unchanged). PerioGT\'s PGFT solves this by making pre-trained representations active conditioning signals during fine-tuning. Any solution must address how periodicity knowledge is UTILIZED, not just ACQUIRED.", "H5 (Architecture-Dependent Periodicity Encoding): NEW — Periodicity encoding via graph topology (periodic edges) is architecture-dependent: beneficial with Chemprop\'s directed bond messages but harmful with GIN\'s atom-level messages. Periodicity must be encoded in a way that matches the architecture\'s capabilities — explicit features for GIN, topology for Chemprop, spectral decomposition for BlochPoly."]', 'daily_journal': [{'day': 2, 'journal': "Day 2 Journal — Polymer Deep Learning Research\n\n**Learning**: Read JEPA (polymer SSL), GPS++ (molecular GPS+denoising), SPSE (path-based structural encoding better than RWSE for cycles).\n\n**Experiments**: (1) GPS-SPSE-Trimer: 54.41 RMSE — FAILED. Small-Graph Paradox: Transformer redundant on 30-60 node polymer trimers where MPNN already reaches all nodes. (2) JEPA pre-training collapsed (EMA+MSE → constant output). (3) Contrastive alternative: 50.77 — close but doesn't beat 50.19. Architecture alone and generic SSL alone both insufficient.\n\n**Internalization**: Architecture-SSL Co-Design Principle — simple MPNN + periodicity-aware contrastive is the only validated path. Three MetaMethod lessons: Small-Graph Paradox, SSL Collapse Prevention, Periodicity Priors Essential.\n\n**Ideas for Day 3**: PCR-v2 (in-training monomer-trimer contrastive), PerioGT-Lite (PA-based pre-training on PI1M), BlochPoly (Bloch theorem spectral decomposition)."}, {'day': 3, 'journal': "Day 3 Journal — Polymer Deep Learning Research\n\n**Learning**: Read Antoniuk et al. (periodic polymer graphs + MPNN, ~20% RMSE reduction) and MoCL (domain-aware contrastive). Key: periodic edges help Chemprop but hurt GIN (architecture-dependent); domain-aware augmentation needs hard positives.\n\n**Experiments**: (1) PCR-v2: FAILED — all variants worse than baseline (best 52.24 vs 51.34). Monomer-trimer pairs are easy positives (cos_sim 0.94-0.97). (2) PerioGT-Lite: FAILED — multi-seed shows no improvement (52.92±0.29 vs 52.49±1.25, p=0.53). Single-seed 49.83 was statistical fluctuation. Pre-training gives variance reduction only, not mean improvement.\n\n**Internalization**: Representation-Usage Gap (H4) — GIN learns periodicity but can't USE it. Architecture-Dependent Periodicity Encoding (H5). Simple GIN + contrastive paradigm EXHAUSTED.\n\n**Ideas for Day 4**: Chemprop-Polymer (architecture-periodicity matching), BlochSpec (computed Bloch band features as input — paradigm shift), PCFT (prompt-conditioned fine-tuning)."}]}, 'connections': {}}]}

