{'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_002', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'MetaMethod', 'keyword': 'MetaMethod', 'content': {'aha moment': "Strive to master the ability of epiphany; when hitting a dead end, step out of the local context and rethink 'what is most important'.", 'research_habits': '["**The proposed algorithm must be sufficiently innovative and original**, and the work of others must be properly cited.", "Analyze why top models excel, maybe integrate their strengths to innovate, **copying is strictly prohibited.**", "From Hypothesis to Experiment: Start with validated or pending hypotheses to design ingenious model structures and experimental strategies. Based on observed real-world results, confirm relevant hypotheses and discover interesting phenomena.", "Match algorithms to physical reality: Consider the actual object behind the data. Models suited for crystals/alloys may not fit p-SMILES polymer datasets. Avoid blind cross-application", "Negative results are valuable — attention and virtual node increasing overfitting is an important finding that prevents wasted effort on similar incremental approaches. Document and internalize negative results with the same rigor as positive ones.", "Simple baselines can be surprisingly strong — GIN+Trimer outperformed all 6 more complex combinations. Always establish simple baselines before adding complexity, and evaluate whether complexity actually improves the overfitting gap (train-test RMSE difference), not just train RMSE.", "Small-Graph Paradox lesson: On graphs where MPNN already reaches all nodes (avg shortest path < MPNN depth), Transformer attention is redundant overhead that increases overfitting without adding predictive value. Match architecture to graph scale — assess reachability before adding global attention. GPS-SPSE-Trimer=54.41 confirmed this on polymer trimers (30-60 nodes).", "Easy vs Hard Positive Pairs lesson: Contrastive learning needs HARD positives (structurally different but semantically equivalent). Monomer-trimer pairs are EASY positives (cos_sim 0.94-0.97) providing redundant signal. Need augmentations that create structural diversity while preserving periodic semantics. MoCL shows domain-aware augmentation is crucial but needs hard positives.", "Architecture-Dependent Periodicity Encoding lesson: Periodic edges benefit Chemprop (bond-level directed messages) but hurt GIN (atom-level messages). Topological periodicity encoding is not universal — must match architecture. Features for GIN, topology for Chemprop, spectral decomposition for BlochPoly.", "Statistical Fluctuation Warning: Single-seed results can show dramatic improvement (-4%) that disappears with multi-seed verification (PerioGT-Lite: single-seed 49.83 vs multi-seed mean 52.92±0.29, p=0.53). Always verify promising results with multiple seeds before drawing conclusions."]', 'anti_self_deception': '["Zero Fabrication: Absolutely no hallucinations or fabrication of non-factual information. If an experiment fails or falls short of expectations, report it honestly; failure is also meaningful.", "The overfitting gap diagnostic: never celebrate lower train RMSE if test RMSE increases. More expressive models that reduce train RMSE but increase test RMSE are memorizing, not learning. The overfitting gap (train RMSE - test RMSE) is the honest metric of generalization.", "SSL Collapse Prevention lesson: JEPA-style EMA+MSE prediction converges to trivial constant-output solution without explicit variance/covariance regularization (VICReg). Contrastive learning (InfoNCE) naturally prevents collapse via negative pairs. For practical pre-training, prefer contrastive approaches over JEPA unless VICReg is carefully implemented. Day 2 JEPA collapse confirmed this.", "Augmentation Difficulty Sweet Spot lesson: Too easy (cos_sim>0.93) = redundant signal. Too hard (cos_sim<0.80) = negative transfer. Sweet spot (0.88-0.93) is narrow, fragile, and non-monotonic. This makes contrastive augmentation tuning extremely difficult for polymer graphs. The relationship between augmentation difficulty and performance is NOT monotonic — there is no reliable gradient to follow.", "Statistical Fluctuation Warning: Single-seed results can show -4% improvement that disappears with multi-seed verification (PerioGT-Lite: 49.83 single-seed vs 52.92±0.29 multi-seed mean, p=0.53). Always verify with ≥3 seeds before claiming improvement. The 49.83 result was a statistical fluctuation, not a real signal.", "Pre-training ≠ Better Representations: PI1M-scale PA contrastive pre-training reduces variance (std 0.29 vs 1.25) but does NOT improve mean performance (52.92 vs 52.49). Pre-training provides mild regularization, not useful periodicity priors for simple GIN. The model can LEARN periodicity-aware representations but cannot USE them — this is the representation-usage gap."]', 'learning_discipline': '["Paper Selection: Select 0-2 papers from the pool and 0-2 papers from arXiv. Do not select duplicate or overly similar papers.", "Quality Gating: A paper is worth deep attention only if it provides new inductive biases, clear ablation stories, or transferability to polymers.", "Periodicity Priors are Essential lesson: Generic SSL (random masking, random contrastive pairs) provides regularization but not domain-specific knowledge. The augmentation strategy must encode the domain\'s physical priors. For polymers, periodic augmentation (different fragments from the same chain) is essential; for other domains, identify and encode the corresponding physical priors. Contrastive pre-training got 50.77 (doesn\'t beat 50.19) — generic SSL is insufficient.", "Implementation Consistency lesson: Pre-training and fine-tuning must use EXACTLY the same architecture (dropout rate, pooling method, normalization). Mismatches cause catastrophic degradation (~50 RMSE points observed). This is a non-negotiable engineering requirement.", "Contrastive Difficulty Trap lesson: Contrastive learning on polymer graphs faces a fundamental difficulty trap. Easy positives (monomer-trimer, cos_sim 0.94-0.97) provide redundant signal. Hard positives (cos_sim<0.80) cause negative transfer. The sweet spot (0.88-0.93) is narrow and fragile. Contrastive learning optimizes embedding similarity, but what matters for polymers is propagation structure — these are different objectives. The simple GIN + contrastive paradigm is EXHAUSTED."]', 'High-ROI Experimentation': "Prioritize performance given moderate hardware (11GB VRAM per GPU). Never under-train to save compute—it masks true algorithmic potential. Maximize Scientific Increment per Compute Unit. When a simple baseline establishes a ceiling (e.g., GIN+Trimer=50.19), and all incremental modifications fail to improve it, shift strategy immediately to fundamentally different approaches rather than continuing to tweak the same architecture family. The ceiling signals that the architecture's expressiveness-regularization balance is wrong, not that minor adjustments will fix it. Day 3 confirmed: PCR-v2 (52.24), PerioGT-Lite (52.92 mean), GPS (54.41), JEPA (collapsed), contrastive (50.77) — ALL failed to beat 50.19. The simple GIN + contrastive paradigm is EXHAUSTED. Pivot from 'how to learn periodicity' to 'how to USE periodicity.' Day 4 options: PGFT (prompt-guided fine-tuning), LiGhT path encoding, Chemprop+periodic edges, BlochPoly spectral decomposition."}, 'connections': {}}]}

