- **Result**: 21/24 improvements over RWSE, strongest on molecular graphs with pure transformers; limited on dense SBM and GPS (restricted to ε)
- **Topic connections**: Directly relevant to H2/H5 — confirms Small-Graph Paradox (GPS-SPSE-Trimer=54.41), validates cycle-counting theory for periodicity encoding, and shows architecture-encoding matching is critical
Skimmed 2024_LONG-SHORT-RANGE_MESSAGE-PASSING_A_PHYSICS-INFORMED_FRAMEWORK_TO_CAPTURE_NON-LOCAL_INTERACTION__2: Now I have a thorough understanding of both the TopicState/MetaMethod and the paper content. Let me carefully analyze and commit this paper to memory.

The LSR-MP paper addresses a fundamental problem: existing EGNNs model only local interactions (within a cutoff radius), losing long-range information (electrostatic, van der Waals) critical for large molecules. Simply increasing cutoff or depth causes over-squashing. The solution is a physics-informed framework that combines fragmentation-based methods (which capture long-range but miss many-body) with EGNNs (which capture many-body but miss long-range), using bipartite atom-fragment message passing.

