### PCFT (Prompt-Conditioned Fine-Tuning): FAILED

**Primary Result**: PCFT with PA pre-trained prompt achieved **52.72 ± 0.76** test RMSE vs baseline **52.46 ± 0.20** (p=0.64, **NOT significant**). The prompt conditioning mechanism does NOT bridge the representation-usage gap.

### Complete Ablation Table

| Ablation | Test RMSE (mean±std) | Train RMSE | Overfitting Gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| (a) GIN+Trimer Baseline | **52.46 ± 0.20** | 22.51 | -29.95 |
| (b) PCFT Random-Init | 53.34 ± 0.36 | 43.47 | -9.87 |
| (c) PCFT PA Pre-trained | 52.72 ± 0.76 | 34.13 | -18.59 |
| (d) Standard Fine-tuning | 53.53 ± 0.37 | 26.70 | -26.83 |
| (e) Both Frozen | 54.02 ± 1.43 | 40.19 | -13.83 |

### Root Cause: Near-Degenerate h_pre

The **critical diagnostic** reveals why PCFT failed: the frozen monomer encoder produces **near-degenerate representations** (mean pairwise cosine similarity = 0.963, 72.8% of pairs > 0.95). All 7170 polymers get nearly identical 256-dim "prompt" vectors. The prompt carries **zero discriminative information** — it cannot condition predictions because every polymer gets the same conditioning signal.

This is a fundamental architectural limitation: **monomer graphs (~10-20 nodes) are too small and too structurally similar** for contrastive pre-training to produce diverse representations. Periodicity is an **inter-RU phenomenon** — it cannot be captured by a single-RU encoder.

### Hypothesis Conclusions

- **H4a NOT SUPPORTED**: Prompt conditioning cannot bridge the representation-usage gap when the prompt itself is degenerate. The mechanism is sound in principle but fails because the source (monomer encoder) is too weak.
- **H4b WEAKLY EVIDENCED**: PCFT reduces overfitting gap (18.59 vs 29.95), suggesting regularization effect, but test RMSE is not improved.
- **New H6 (Monomer Encoder Degeneracy)**: Monomer-level GIN encoders produce near-degenerate representations regardless of pre-training. Periodicity requires inter-RU encoding.

### Overfitting Gap Insight

PCFT consistently reduces overfitting gap (regularization effect from frozen prompt), but this comes at the cost of reduced capacity (train RMSE increases from 22.51 to 34.13). **Reducing overfitting gap does NOT guarantee better test RMSE** — the model needs both regularization AND capacity.

### Next Steps

The monomer-level prompt is fundamentally insufficient. Three viable paths remain:
1. **BlochSpec** — shift periodicity from learned (degenerate) representations to computed (discriminative) spectral input features
2. **Chemprop-Polymer** — architecture-periodicity matching with directed bond messages on periodic graphs
3. **Trimer-level dual-encoder** — both channels process trimer graphs (but removes the monomer-trimer asymmetry PCFT relies on)

