{'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_002', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'MetaMethod', 'keyword': 'MetaMethod', 'content': {'aha moment': "Strive to master the ability of epiphany; when hitting a dead end, step out of the local context and rethink 'what is most important'.", 'research_habits': '["**The proposed algorithm must be sufficiently innovative and original**, and the work of others must be properly cited.", "Analyze why top models excel, maybe integrate their strengths to innovate, **copying is strictly prohibited.**", "From Hypothesis to Experiment: Start with validated or pending hypotheses to design ingenious model structures and experimental strategies. Based on observed real-world results, confirm relevant hypotheses and discover interesting phenomena.", "Match algorithms to physical reality: Consider the actual object behind the data. Models suited for crystals/alloys may not fit p-SMILES polymer datasets. Avoid blind cross-application", "Negative results are valuable — attention and virtual node increasing overfitting is an important finding that prevents wasted effort on similar incremental approaches. Document and internalize negative results with the same rigor as positive ones.", "Simple baselines can be surprisingly strong — GIN+Trimer outperformed all 6 more complex combinations. Always establish simple baselines before adding complexity, and evaluate whether complexity actually improves the overfitting gap (train-test RMSE difference), not just train RMSE.", "Small-Graph Paradox lesson: On graphs where MPNN already reaches all nodes (avg shortest path < MPNN depth), Transformer attention is redundant overhead that increases overfitting without adding predictive value. Match architecture to graph scale — assess reachability before adding global attention. GPS-SPSE-Trimer=54.41 confirmed this on polymer trimers (30-60 nodes).", "Easy vs Hard Positive Pairs lesson: Contrastive learning needs HARD positives (structurally different but semantically equivalent). Monomer-trimer pairs are EASY positives (cos_sim 0.94-0.97) providing redundant signal. Need augmentations that create structural diversity while preserving periodic semantics. MoCL shows domain-aware augmentation is crucial but needs hard positives.", "Architecture-Dependent Periodicity Encoding lesson: Periodic edges benefit Chemprop (bond-level directed messages) but hurt GIN (atom-level messages). Topological periodicity encoding is not universal — must match architecture. Features for GIN, topology for Chemprop, spectral decomposition for BlochPoly.", "Statistical Fluctuation Warning: Single-seed results can show dramatic improvement (-4%) that disappears with multi-seed verification (PerioGT-Lite: single-seed 49.83 vs multi-seed mean 52.92±0.29, p=0.53). Always verify promising results with multiple seeds before drawing conclusions.", "Degenerate Prompt Warning: Prompt conditioning fails when the source encoder produces near-degenerate representations (PCFT monomer encoder cos_sim=0.963). Always check prompt discriminability before relying on conditioning mechanisms. A conditioning signal that cannot distinguish between inputs is useless regardless of how sophisticated the conditioning mechanism is.", "Feature Injection Redundancy Principle: Graph-level feature injection is redundant with message passing on sufficiently large graphs. BlochSpec confirmed: random features match real features, unweighted matches weighted. The GNN already captures what appended features encode. Always include random feature controls to verify that injected features provide genuinely new information, not just redundant signals that the model already has.", "HOW-vs-WHAT Principle: The 50→30 gap requires changing the MESSAGE PASSING MECHANISM (HOW periodicity flows through the graph), not adding features/prompts/pre-training (WHAT information the model receives). Three paradigms that change WHAT are exhausted: contrastive pre-training, GIN architecture innovation, graph-level feature injection. All produce 50-54 RMSE. Only changing HOW — via node-level periodicity injection during MP, directed bond messages on periodic edges, or equivariant MP — can bridge the gap."]', 'anti_self_deception': '["Zero Fabrication: Absolutely no hallucinations or fabrication of non-factual information. If an experiment fails or falls short of expectations, report it honestly; failure is also meaningful.", "The overfitting gap diagnostic: never celebrate lower train RMSE if test RMSE increases. More expressive models that reduce train RMSE but increase test RMSE are memorizing, not learning. The overfitting gap (train RMSE - test RMSE) is the honest metric of generalization.", "SSL Collapse Prevention lesson: JEPA-style EMA+MSE prediction converges to trivial constant-output solution without explicit variance/covariance regularization (VICReg). Contrastive learning (InfoNCE) naturally prevents collapse via negative pairs. For practical pre-training, prefer contrastive approaches over JEPA unless VICReg is carefully implemented. Day 2 JEPA collapse confirmed this.", "Augmentation Difficulty Sweet Spot lesson: Too easy (cos_sim>0.93) = redundant signal. Too hard (cos_sim<0.80) = negative transfer. Sweet spot (0.88-0.93) is narrow, fragile, and non-monotonic. This makes contrastive augmentation tuning extremely difficult for polymer graphs. The relationship between augmentation difficulty and performance is NOT monotonic — there is no reliable gradient to follow.", "Statistical Fluctuation Warning: Single-seed results can show -4% improvement that disappears with multi-seed verification (PerioGT-Lite: 49.83 single-seed vs 52.92±0.29 multi-seed mean, p=0.53). Always verify with ≥3 seeds before claiming improvement. The 49.83 result was a statistical fluctuation, not a real signal.", "Pre-training ≠ Better Representations: PI1M-scale PA contrastive pre-training reduces variance (std 0.29 vs 1.25) but does NOT improve mean performance (52.92 vs 52.49). Pre-training provides mild regularization, not useful periodicity priors for simple GIN. The model can LEARN periodicity-aware representations but cannot USE them — this is the representation-usage gap.", "Correlation ≠ Usefulness: BlochSpec features had r=-0.64 correlation with Tg target, yet were completely redundant with existing GNN capacity (random features matched real features). High target correlation does NOT mean a feature provides new information — it may encode the same structure the model already captures through message passing. Always include random feature controls.", "Regularization-Capacity Trade-off: Reducing overfitting gap through capacity removal (fewer parameters, simpler model) is counterproductive unless it comes with genuinely discriminative information. BlochSpec\'s unweighted (fewer parameters) matched weighted — the improvement was from removing redundant capacity, not from adding useful information. Capacity reduction without informative content is just making a smaller bad model."]', 'learning_discipline': '["Paper Selection: Select 0-2 papers from the pool and 0-2 papers from arXiv. Do not select duplicate or overly similar papers.", "Quality Gating: A paper is worth deep attention only if it provides new inductive biases, clear ablation stories, or transferability to polymers.", "Periodicity Priors are Essential lesson: Generic SSL (random masking, random contrastive pairs) provides regularization but not domain-specific knowledge. The augmentation strategy must encode the domain\'s physical priors. For polymers, periodic augmentation (different fragments from the same chain) is essential; for other domains, identify and encode the corresponding physical priors. Contrastive pre-training got 50.77 (doesn\'t beat 50.19) — generic SSL is insufficient.", "Implementation Consistency lesson: Pre-training and fine-tuning must use EXACTLY the same architecture (dropout rate, pooling method, normalization). Mismatches cause catastrophic degradation (~50 RMSE points observed). This is a non-negotiable engineering requirement.", "Three Exhausted Paradigms lesson: (1) Contrastive pre-training — provides variance reduction only, not mean improvement. Easy positives (cos_sim 0.94-0.97) provide redundant signal. (2) GIN architecture innovation — GAT, VN, GPS all increase overfitting without improving test RMSE. GIN+Trimer=50.19 is the architectural ceiling. (3) Graph-level feature injection — redundant with message passing (BlochSpec: random features match real features). All three paradigms produce 50-54 RMSE. The 50→30 gap requires fundamentally different message passing mechanisms, not incremental improvements within exhausted paradigms."]', 'High-ROI Experimentation': 'Prioritize performance given moderate hardware (11GB VRAM per GPU). Never under-train to save compute—it masks true algorithmic potential. Maximize Scientific Increment per Compute Unit. THREE paradigms are now EXHAUSTED (Day 4 confirmed): (1) Contrastive pre-training, (2) GIN architecture innovation, (3) Graph-level feature injection — all produce 50-54 RMSE. PCFT failed (degenerate monomer prompts, cos_sim=0.963). BlochSpec failed (spectral features redundant with MP). The 50→30 gap requires changing the MESSAGE PASSING MECHANISM (HOW), not adding information (WHAT). Day 5 pivot to message-passing mechanism change: (A) PolymerBipartite — RU virtual nodes injecting periodicity at node level during MP (adapts LSR-MP validated bipartite paradigm), (B) Chemprop-Polymer — directed bond messages on periodic edges, (C) Equivariant MP with periodicity-conditioned attention. Mandatory diagnostics for all candidates: measure RU virtual node pairwise cos_sim (<0.90 required, not >0.95 degenerate), include random feature controls, verify improvements from mechanism change not parameter count, check overfitting gap reduction comes from informative content not capacity removal.'}, 'connections': {}}]}

