Actually, I realize I should think even more carefully about what makes this truly concept-leaping vs. just a different technical approach. The key question is: WHY would KRR on embeddings outperform the FFN that was trained end-to-end?

The answer lies in the **entanglement problem**: In end-to-end training, the encoder and FFN are jointly optimized. The encoder learns to produce embeddings that the FFN can easily map to Tg. But the FFN is a simple 2-layer network — it can only learn smooth, relatively simple mappings. So the encoder is incentivized to produce embeddings where Tg is a smooth function of the embedding dimensions. This means the encoder may NOT learn the most informative representation — it learns the representation that is easiest for the FFN to use, which may be a suboptimal representation.

When we decouple the encoder from the predictor:
- The encoder can be trained to produce maximally informative embeddings (via multi-task learning on diverse properties)
- The kernel method can then learn a complex nonlinear mapping from these embeddings to Tg
- The kernel method is not constrained to learn smooth mappings — it can learn discontinuous, piecewise-linear, or any complex mapping through the kernel function

This is analogous to how in NLP, pre-trained language models produce general representations, and then different downstream tasks use different prediction mechanisms (linear probes, fine-tuning, etc.) on the same representations. The representation is decoupled from the task-specific predictor.

